 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server commits each edit as one event revision and pushes committed revisions to connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets. Browsers display a revisioned view of the board and replay the revisions they missed after a reconnect. Subsecond updates are the target; the required freshness bound is 15 seconds.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around this route. Socket delivery needs managed connection sessions, a replayable revision log, and reliable publication of each committed revision to the sessions that should receive it. The board takes on the operating work of connection capacity and reconnect handling in exchange for fast updates and no dependency on a realtime vendor.
 
